Marfan Syndrome (CV Manifestations)
Cardiovascular
Marfanoid habitus/Arachnodactyly, Ectopia lentis, Diastolic murmur of aortic regurgitation, Aortic root dilation on echo

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CDV-026
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of severe, tearing chest pain radiating to the back in a patient with a known connective tissue disorder.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and roommate
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a young adult with sudden, excruciating, tearing chest and back pain. Given the patient's known Marfanoid phenotype, this presentation is a pathognomonic red flag for acute aortic dissection or rupture. Immediate in-person assessment was mandated to evaluate hemodynamic stability, assess pulse deficits, and arrange immediate emergency transport to prevent catastrophic hemorrhagic death, strictly precluding any delay until routine hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "It feels like my chest and back are physically tearing apart from the inside, and the pain is unbearable."
History of Present Illness: Patient (aged 28) reports the sudden onset of severe, tearing, maximal-at-onset chest pain that radiates directly through to the interscapular region of the back. The pain is relentless and unaffected by movement or respiration. The patient feels profoundly lightheaded and nauseous. Known history of joint hypermobility and visual issues (ectopia lentis). KEY SYMPTOMS: Sudden onset tearing chest pain radiating to the back indicative of aortic dissection. Marfanoid habitus with excessively long extremities and arachnodactyly. Ectopia lentis or superior lens dislocation. Early diastolic murmur of aortic regurgitation (newly symptomatic).
Functional Status: Normally independent, currently writhing in agony on the floor.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of imminent cardiovascular collapse.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: Right Arm: 160/90, Left Arm: 110/60 | HR: 115 | Temp: 36.5 C | RR: 24 | SpO2: 95 percent | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Profoundly distressed, diaphoretic, striking Marfanoid habitus (tall stature, long limbs).
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Significant blood pressure differential (>20 mmHg) between right and left arms. Tachycardic rhythm. A new, blowing early diastolic murmur of aortic regurgitation is audible at the right upper sternal border. Widened pulse pressure on the right. Radial pulse is palpably diminished on the left compared to the right. Musculoskeletal/Skin: Arachnodactyly (positive wrist and thumb signs), pectus excavatum deformity. HEENT: High arched palate. DIAGNOSTIC TETRAD: Marfanoid habitus/Arachnodactyly, Ectopia lentis (by history), Diastolic murmur of aortic regurgitation (present), Aortic root dilation on echo (presumed ruptured/dissected pending emergency imaging).
Environmental Observations: Patient unable to stand or sit still due to pain.
Medication Reconciliation: Prescribed beta-blockers for known aortic root dilation, but compliance has been poor.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Aortic Dissection (secondary to Marfan Syndrome) - Status: Critical Emergency
Differentials:

Acute Myocardial Infarction - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The specific "tearing" nature of the pain radiating to the back, rather than crushing retrosternal pain radiating to the jaw/arm, combined with dramatic asymmetrical pulses and BP readings.

Pulmonary Embolism - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Pain is tearing rather than pleuritic, and the presence of a new aortic diastolic murmur with pulse deficits points directly to the aorta.

Musculoskeletal Back Spasm - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The acute, maximal-at-onset systemic hemodynamic compromise (pulse deficits, BP asymmetry) and new cardiac murmur.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the classic triad of acute aortic dissection: tearing chest/back pain, pulse deficits/BP asymmetry, and a new murmur of aortic regurgitation, all set against the high-risk background of Marfan syndrome. This is a supreme surgical emergency carrying a massive mortality rate per hour of delay. An urgent after-hours physical assessment was critical to identify the asymmetrical pulses and new murmur, definitively differentiating this from a simple musculoskeletal strain, and immediately triggering an emergency ambulance dispatch with pre-notification to the cardiothoracic surgical center.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediate emergency ambulance dispatch. Contacted the emergency department and on-call cardiothoracic registrar to alert them of a highly probable acute Type A aortic dissection requiring immediate CT aortogram and surgical intervention.
Positive Behavioral Change: Instructed the patient to remain completely flat and completely still on the floor, and explicitly ordered them to focus only on slow, shallow breathing. By forcing absolute physical stillness and minimizing respiratory effort, the patient actively reduces sheer stress on the tearing aortic wall. The reward is a slight stabilization of their heart rate to delay further catastrophic rupture while awaiting paramedics.
Home-Based Services: None.
DME/Safety: None.
Follow-up: Acute cardiothoracic admission and emergency surgery anticipated.

